From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: read and translate a picture subtitle in one
 request

A subtitle made of pictures opened to be translated took two requests
a batch: the model read the pictures, then the cue stage's worker sent
the text it read to the model again to translate it. A local model
reads three or four lines in ten seconds that way, less than dense
dialogue is spoken.

When the track is opened to be translated (hints.translateTo), the
reader now asks for both at once: the image of the stacked pictures,
with what the video is, its characters, the lines read before (context
only, dropped at a seek), and to answer each numbered row with what it
reads, " => ", and its translation. The context is the one the
translation worker is given, now built by AI::TranslationContext() for
both.

An answer is used when every row comes back numbered once with both
halves. A row whose translation is its own text again - case and
spacing aside, the model answering in the source language - or is far
longer than it (the check a translated line gets) goes to the cue
stage as it was read, to be translated there: the first half is the
reading, with no further request. Each other row's translation is laid
on two lines as a translated line is.

An answer of no use - a row left out or without its two halves, a
remark, a refusal, an error from the provider - or none in time has
the same image asked again to be read only, and the text read goes
through the cue stage as before: repaired, then translated by its
worker. A line is never lost to the combined request. A request
dropped by a seek or the stream closing is not asked again. After
three answers of no use in a row, or two requests with no answer in
time (a model too slow to do both within the provider's silence), the
reader stops asking for both and only reads.

A line read and translated at once is not repaired: the repair fixes
the mistakes of OCR in the source language - its dictionaries are that
language's - and the model reading the picture makes none of those; on
a translation it would only do harm. Its ad removal does not apply to
those lines either. A line read only, or left untranslated, is
repaired as before.

Translated lines reach the screen the way the others do: the cue
stage's new Pass() hands them to its worker, which delivers them in
their turn among the cues it translates, untouched, and drops them at
a seek like any other (CCueWorker::Pass; the queue keeps a batch of
cues worked on already apart from one to work on). What the model read
of them joins the worker's lines before, as the lines it translates
itself do, so a line translated after them has them as context. They
go through the codec's Show(): escaped for libass, placed where the
picture was, moved when late, and logged "line at X s ready Y s ahead
of it".

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>

